WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.070
- You're sitting in
your office and you know

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:03.660
that for the next day
you still need to create

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:08.350
an extensive slide presentation
of 10, 20, 30 pages.

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:09.183
So what do you do?

00:00:09.183 --> 00:00:11.640
You just work all night to do that?

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.410
Do you want to think about the layout

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.120
of every single slide in an extensive way?

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:19.080
No, this is surely not what
I would recommend you to do.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:19.913
What you need

00:00:19.913 --> 00:00:23.333
are some very good design
and layout principles

00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:26.800
that you can rely on to just produce.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.450
Just produce the deck, we
don't need to think too much

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:34.000
about how every individual
slide needs to look like.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.800
This is exactly what I wanna
talk about to you today.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.480
My name is Heinrich, I'm a
former McKinsey Consultant

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.350
and on my channel I want to help you

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:44.460
to create slide presentations
in the same style

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:45.700
the leading consulting firms

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.070
like Boston Consulting Group or Bain do.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:50.310
And today we're going to
talk about slide layouts

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:51.690
and throughout the
video we're going to see

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:56.020
lots of example slides and I
will offer all of those to you

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.240
as a download as an open PowerPoint file

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.780
so you can use them for
yourself as a template.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:04.350
There's a download link to
them in the video description

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:05.570
feel free to check them out.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:08.140
And before we jump in, I wanna
say thank you to Think Cell

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.353
for sponsoring this video.

00:01:09.353 --> 00:01:13.250
Think Cell is a tool that I
literally used almost every day

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.470
to improve my PowerPoint slides.

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.490
You will hear more about them later today.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:19.790
The key principle I
want to talk about today

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.570
is that every slide is a table.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:25.100
When creating slides,
you should always think

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:29.210
about organizing information
on the page in a table format.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:31.830
So what I do not mean by that
is that you should indeed

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:33.785
just insert a table in PowerPoint

00:01:33.785 --> 00:01:36.260
as maybe it looks like here on the slide.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.070
But instead you should
always consider your page

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:43.250
being a collection of rows and columns,

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:46.864
and always organize your
information in this framework.

00:01:46.864 --> 00:01:51.520
So every slide is a table, but
what exactly does this mean?

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.560
So in order to make this
a little bit more tangible

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.660
I will structure this video
along three main types

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:57.540
of slides.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:02.240
First qualitative slides,
second quantitative slides

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.170
and third integrated slides.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:07.070
We want to integrate both qualitative

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:09.890
and quantitative information.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:12.260
So let's directly jump into the section

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.410
on qualitative slides.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:15.500
And I'm gonna always start

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.330
by first showing you a
general slide blueprint,

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:19.950
and then some actual examples

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.637
so you can bring this format to life.

00:02:21.637 --> 00:02:25.550
You'll see a basic layout for
a qualitative slide like that.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:28.640
I hope you directly
recognize the table format.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.540
In the columns you have the
different types of information

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:33.810
that you want to talk about.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.580
And this is one key thought

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:38.870
that whenever you have
different types of information,

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:40.920
you will include that
in different columns.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.930
This is an error that
many people are doing.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.190
They're just having a page

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:46.030
just with a list of bullet points.

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:47.790
And then maybe the first bullet point

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:49.430
is about a general principle.

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:52.330
The second bullet point
is about an example.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.720
The third bullet point
is about a suggestion

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.840
that you are making, you
are mixing different types

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.500
of logical information,
different logical types of data.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.700
You should always then separate
this in different columns.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.350
Then in the rows, you will
typically present different types

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:10.094
of information that you apply
the things that you talk about

00:03:10.094 --> 00:03:11.370
in the headers to.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.430
The general idea is to avoid
just a long list of bullets

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:17.100
to avoid these so-called cloth lines.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:18.620
If you have no idea what a cloth line is

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:19.770
and why you should avoid it.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:21.790
I created a video about that in the past.

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:24.230
I will link it somewhere above
here for you to reference.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:25.820
It's really a key concept.

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:27.210
Check it out if you haven't seen it yet.

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.160
So for the theory,

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.770
let's look at a couple
of practical examples.

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:33.160
So here you have a version
of this very slides,

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.770
we have first the column for the measures.

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:37.550
So the different measures
that you're proposing

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.640
and then on the right
column for the rationale.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:42.090
So why do you think
this measure is needed?

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:46.000
And you have nicely organized
all these measures in rows.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.640
Of course, this is a very
wordy, a very text heavy slide.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.430
So likely not something for your main deck

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.200
but maybe something for appendix.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.888
The think it nicely
illustrates the principle

00:03:55.888 --> 00:03:58.720
with this table format, you
can very nicely aggregate

00:03:58.720 --> 00:03:59.820
and show this data.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.070
Next there may be a little
bit more complicated version

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:03.930
of such a page of a first column

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.260
with the name of the
recommendation that you have,

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:07.920
a second column with the details.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.070
What exactly is it that
you're recommending.

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:12.070
A third column with the average impact

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:15.370
that maybe you are estimating
to include it as a number

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:18.180
and then an additional
column for the roadmap,

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:19.880
for the timing of it all.

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:22.960
Again, notice how different
types of information

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:25.090
are stretched out to different columns.

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:28.060
And then the logical elements
that you want to talk about

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:29.520
are organized in the row.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.690
This is a very typical
standard slide format

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:34.920
that you will be able to
create in a quite quick

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:36.440
and straightforward way.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:38.050
And again, all these slides

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:39.430
that I will show you here in this video

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:42.720
offered to you as a download
in an open PowerPoint format,

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.290
check out the link in
the video description

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:45.770
if this is of interest to you.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.560
Next, just an additional layout idea

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:49.250
to illustrate that you don't always

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:51.760
need to follow this strict column format

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:53.070
with the column headers.

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:55.970
Of course, for the columns, you
can also use other elements.

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:57.613
For instance, here on this example,

00:04:57.613 --> 00:05:00.950
a timeline or maybe also process chart

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:02.980
with different process bars

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:04.960
that you could use for the columns.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:06.840
So you could of course play with it,

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:08.760
include some creativity in it.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:10.390
So the slides surely do not need to look

00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:12.950
all the same and you
can mix it up as needed.

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:14.870
Next let's look at quantitative charts.

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:17.370
So charts that have some
data visualization on it,

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:19.690
some charts, some diagrams on it.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:21.410
Of course this is a very common chart

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:23.798
and probably the majority
of all the charts

00:05:23.798 --> 00:05:25.966
that you will create, at least
in professional services.

00:05:25.966 --> 00:05:28.690
So let's look what you can
do with the layouts in here.

