00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:18.400
Let's now jump into quantitative slides.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:22.170
What you see here on this
page is arguably the mother

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:25.050
of all quantitative slide layouts.

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:26.860
And again, I hope that
you directly recognize

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:27.800
the table format.

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:30.130
So on the first column, we have a column

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:32.100
on the methodology that you applied here

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:33.390
for your data analysis.

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:35.489
And the second column, we have the chart

00:07:35.489 --> 00:07:38.350
with the data visualization
that you want to highlight.

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:40.680
And then in the third
column we have a column

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.560
on the implications of this page.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:46.840
What is the so what of this page.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:50.730
Frankly, easily 50% of
all of the pages I created

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:53.310
in consulting followed some version

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:55.480
of this very basic layout.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:58.500
And in many cases you don't
even need the first column

00:07:58.500 --> 00:07:59.870
on the methodology.

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:02.310
You have more space than for the chart,

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:03.900
for the data visualization configured out

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:07.180
and then focus on the right
with a couple of bullets.

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:09.050
What implications you need are,

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:11.720
what the reader, recipient
of your presentation

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:14.750
is actually supposed to
take away from this chart.

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:16.500
But let's look at a couple of examples.

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:18.240
This will be a very basic layout

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:20.120
following exactly this methodology.

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:22.510
So on the left, you have the diagram

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:25.970
showcasing in the bubble
chart format the market size,

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:28.080
the growth, the profitability, the margin

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:29.240
of certain regions.

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:31.580
And then on the right,
you have a simple column

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:35.010
highlighting a couple of
points, a couple of key insights

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:36.881
that you want to
communicate to the reader.

00:08:36.881 --> 00:08:39.100
Here again, this layout is very basic,

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:41.740
but I hope you agree if you
produce a slide like that,

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:43.050
you will never get the feedback

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:44.566
that this is not looking professional.

00:08:44.566 --> 00:08:46.707
This is a very solid page,

00:08:46.707 --> 00:08:48.842
always just following
this very basic layout

00:08:48.842 --> 00:08:50.460
that we just discussed.

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:53.070
Next example, here is
simple waterfall chart.

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:55.780
Because the truth is that
sometimes you don't even need

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:57.800
the additional column on the right.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:01.097
Maybe the chart out of itself
is already self explanatory

00:09:01.097 --> 00:09:04.120
or maybe you just want to
highlight a couple of points

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:05.250
within the chart.

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:06.950
Then you can do it with
this little element

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:08.010
that you see right here.

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:10.331
This is something that is
sometimes called a call out.

00:09:10.331 --> 00:09:13.720
So just the text box
with two line elements

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:16.980
to showcase that also just
the variation of this layout

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:18.010
that we just discussed.

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:20.250
Let's now move to the third type of slide.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:21.930
And these are integrated slides

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:24.380
and these are arguably
the most interesting ones

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:27.820
because the truth is that this
is a style of creating slides

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:30.730
that I think is very very
common in professional services

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:32.790
in consulting, if you work for a firm

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:34.530
like McKinsey, BCG, Bain,

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:37.770
but it's not that often
seen outside of consulting.

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:39.300
And this is a style where you're trying

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:42.010
to integrate the diagram, the chart

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:44.820
even more into your table with the text.

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:47.050
So earlier we've seen
this very simple example

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:49.510
where maybe on the left,
you have a chart or diagram

00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:52.600
and then on the right list
of bullets with implications.

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:54.269
And again, this is surely, okay

00:09:54.269 --> 00:09:57.640
you can do that, but now the
problem maybe, the downside

00:09:57.640 --> 00:09:59.840
is that now there's a certain disconnect

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:02.060
between your bullets and the chart.

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:04.080
People now need to read the bullets

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:05.910
and maybe they didn't find
something interesting.

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:07.650
Now they need to look at the chart

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:09.660
and now they need to try to make the link.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:10.630
And especially if the chart's

00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:13.690
maybe a little bit more complex
this is often not so easy.

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:17.080
And it for sure takes
additional cognitive energy

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:19.520
of the person looking
at this to make sense

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:21.570
out of all the information
that are presented here.

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:24.110
And so the idea of these integrated charts

00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:26.200
and let me maybe directly
jump to an example

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:28.930
is to really integrate the chart

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:32.150
together with additional
bullets with other information

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:33.330
into the table structure.

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:34.470
Again, here you see a chart

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:35.800
where on the left you have a title

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:37.940
and then you have a simple bar chart

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:41.080
then a column with details
and then additional column

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:42.615
with strategic assessment.

00:10:42.615 --> 00:10:44.680
And also notice how this additional column

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:46.650
out there on the right
is in the end of the day,

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:48.970
nothing else than an additional column

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:50.650
that was inserted there.

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:52.930
So always think about
whether it makes sense,

00:10:52.930 --> 00:10:56.370
whether there's an opportunity
to include such a diagram

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:57.930
into your table structure.

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:01.490
And don't just have two
very separated columns

00:11:01.490 --> 00:11:03.300
where then on the left, you have a diagram

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:05.110
on the right you have a list of bullets.

00:11:05.110 --> 00:11:06.810
Of course, this doesn't always make sense,

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:08.470
but often there is a good opportunity

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:09.510
to do something like this.

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:11.230
And then this really helps the reader

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:13.860
to just process the information
in a more direct way.

